cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  What a sequence model appears to contribute is set by how the
  negatives were built
message: >-
  If you use this software or its released evidence, please cite
  the preprint. The DOI below is a placeholder until the Zenodo
  deposit exists; SUBMISSION.md tracks that as the last
  outstanding release step.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Nirmalkumar
    family-names: Thirupallikrishnan Kesavan
    email: thirupallikrishnan.n@northeastern.edu
    affiliation: Northeastern University
repository-code: 'https://github.com/NirmalKumar31/rbp-protocol-calibration'
abstract: >-
  A calibration study across 94 paired ENCODE eCLIP datasets.
  Holding the model, the peak set, the chromosome-blocked folds
  and the estimator fixed and varying only how negative windows
  are constructed moves a model's measured nested contribution
  5.42-fold, while its apparent AUROC moves the opposite way. The
  estimator also returns a positive increment for a model whose
  information the composition baseline already contains, where
  the true contribution is zero by construction, and that floor
  is 90.4 percent of the smallest protocol's reported value.
keywords:
  - RNA-binding proteins
  - eCLIP
  - negative sets
  - benchmark calibration
  - sequence models
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
